<section xml:id="section-groups-properties">
  <title>First Properties of Groups</title>
  <p>
    Having defined a group and given a decent number of examples, I
    can now move back to developing the general theory. This section
    contains a number of useful definitions as well as basic
    propositions that help to understand how a group is structured.
    I'll start with some direct clarifications of the group axioms
    themselves. 
  </p>
  <p>
    When talking about groups in general, it is conventional to write
    the binary operation as a multiplication and talk about products.
    In specific settings, of course, this might not be the case, but
    we have adopted these choices for the general case. Just remember
    that writing <m>ab</m> or talking about <sq>multiplication</sq>
    and <sq>product</sq> might mean something very different if I went
    to an example. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The identity element of a group is unique. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Assume that both <m>e</m> and <m>f</m> are identity elements
        of a group <m>G</m>. Both being the identity  means that
        multiplication by either has no effect: this is the definition
        of an identity. This observation allows me to write the
        following equation.
        <md>
          e = ef = f 
        </md>
          The first equality recognizes that multiplication by
          <m>f</m> has no effect, and the second equality recognizes
          that multiplication by <m>e</m> has no effect. Together, the
          two equalities prove the claim: the two identities must, in
          fact, be the same group element. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-unique-inverses">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Inverse elements in groups are unique. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m>g \in G</m> be a group element. Assume that both
        <m>h</m> and <m>k</m> are inverse elements of <m>g</m>. By
        definition, this means that multiplying <m>g</m> by either
        element (in either order) produces the identity. Using this
        fact, I can write the following equation. 
        <md>
          h = h \Id = h(gk) = (hg)k = \Id k = k 
        </md>
        I'm using the assumption of the proof (that both of these are
        inverses). I'm also using the associativity of the binary
        operation in the middle step. I conclude that the two inverses
        must be the same group element, so inverses are also unique. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    In the next few propositions, I want to talk about algebra of groups,
    in the high-school sense of <sq>algebra</sq>: solving equations
    and using variables. Since groups have a binary operation,
    algebraic equations and variables make sense. However, groups can
    consist of many different kinds of mathematical objects. It's not
    clear, at the start, what parts of algebra still work. Therefore,
    we should re-establish what rules still hold. One of the
    challenges of abstraction is realizing that the old rules you are
    used to may, in fact, fail in new settings. Thankfully, the
    following propositions show that some algebraic manipulation in
    groups still works. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The cancellation law holds in groups. Specifically, consider
        this equation of elements of some group. 
        <md>
          gh = gk
        </md>
        Then I can cancel <m>g</m> and conclude that <m>h = k</m>. The
        same is true for the following equation.
        <md>
          hg = kg
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof here is pretty straightforward: just multiply on the
        left by the inverse and then reduce <m>g^{-1}g</m> to the
        identify (which is the definition of an inverse). 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            gh = gk 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}gh = g^{-1}gk 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \Id h = \Id k 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            h = k 
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The proof for the second equation is precisely the same steps
        just multiplying by the inverse on the right. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    So cancellation works, which is great, since it is a very natural
    and instinctual operation. However, care is still required here:
    cancellation only works if the cancelled element is on the same
    side of both products. Consider this equation of elements of some
    group. 
    <md>
      gh = kg
    </md>
    In general, this <m>g</m> cannot be cancelled. For an example,
    consider this equation in <m>D_4</m>.
    <md>
      F_3 R_3 = R_1 F_3 
    </md>
    From <xref ref="example-d4"/>, I can check that both
    of these product are equal to <m>F_4</m>. However, if I could
    cancel <m>F_3</m>, I would produce the false equality <m>R_3 =
    R_1</m>. So care is required for non-abelian groups and
    cancellation. (If the group is abelian, of course,
    then I can change the order of equations and this cancellation
    would be fine.) 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g,h \in G</m> and let <m>x</m> be a variable that
        stands for an unknown element of the same group. Then the
        equation
        <md>
          gx = h
        </md>
        always has a unique solution in the group <m>G</m>. Likewise,
        the equation 
        <md>
          xg = h
        </md>
        also has a unique solution. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof here is, again, just multiplication by
        <m>g^{-1}</m>. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            gx = h 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}gx = g^{-1}h
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            x = g^{-1}h
          </mrow>
        </md>
        The equation is solved by <m>g^{-1}h</m>. Since inverses are
        unique by <xref ref="proposition-unique-inverses"/>, this
        solution to the equation is a unique solution. The second
        equation is solved in the same manner: I just multiply on the
        right instead of on the left. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Let me give a couple of propositions for working with inverses. 
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-inverse-product">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g \in G</m>. Then <m>(g^{-1})^{-1} = g</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This proposition say that the inverse of <m>g^{-1}</m> is g. I
        alreay know that the inverse of <m>g</m> is <m>g^{-1}</m>,
        which, by definition, means the following. 
        <md>
         g g^{-1} = g^{-1}g = \Id 
        </md>
        However, the same equation reinterpreted means exactly that
        <m>g</m> is the inverse of <m>g^{-1}</m>, which proves the
        claim. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g,h \in G</m>. Then <m>(gh)^{-1} = h^{-1}
        g^{-1}</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        To prove this, I have to prove that <m>h^{-1}g^{-1}</m> is the
        inverse of <m>gh</m>, which means I have to show that the
        multiplication by the potential inverse, on both sides,
        produces the identity. I'll first do multiplication on the
        left. 
        <md>
          h^{-1}g^{-1}gh = h^{-1}\Id h = h^{-1}h = \Id 
        </md>
        Similarly, I can do multiplication on the right.
        <md>
          ghh^{-1}g^{-1} = g \Id g^{-1} = gg^{-1} = \Id 
        </md>
        In both equations, I have used the fact that the inverses of
        <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> produce the identity. Both equations
        together justify the proposition. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    When groups are written with a multiplicative product,
    exponentials can be used for iterated products: <m>g^n = gg \ldots
    g</m>. Since we have inverses, we can allow <m>n \in \ZZ</m> for
    these exponents. With this notations, the laws of exponents still
    apply. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g, h \in G</m>  and let <m>m,n \in \ZZ</m>. Then the
        following equations are true. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            g^m g^n = g^{n+m}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            (g^m)^n = g^{nm}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        In addition, if <m>G</m> is an abelian group, the following
        equation is true. 
        <md>
          (gh)^n = g^n h^n 
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the first two is just counting now many copies of
        <m>g</m> or <m>g^{-1}</m> are in the expression and verifying
        that the count is the same on both sides. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For the third equation, I can expand the left side as <m>n</m>
        iterated multiplications. 
        <md>
          (gh)(gh)(gh) \ldots (gh)
        </md>
        If <m>G</m> is abelian, then I can rearrange this product,
        grouping all the <m>g</m>`s together at the start. 
        <md>
          (gh)(gh)(gh) \ldots (gh) = g^nh^h
        </md>
        The abelian assumption is important: in general, this exponent
        law is not true for non-abelian groups. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Already in the previous proposition there are new subtleties. Most
    of the exponent laws work, but the third needs the additional
    assumption of an abelian groups. This is, again, how we expect the
    process of abstraction to work: the familiar patterns extend, but
    that extension can get subtle and complicated. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Other exponential ideas may or may not extend. If we have exponents,
    we can also ask about logarithms. Given group elements <m>g</m>
    and <m>h</m>, to solve the equation <m>g^n = m</m> is equivalent
    to a logarithm. For finite groups, this is often called a
    <term>discrete logarithm</term> and is quite important in
    cryptography. For a general group and general elements, there is
    no guarantee that a logarithm is solvable.
  </p>
  <p>
    A similar problem is roots, which can be expressed as fractional
    exponents. For <m>g</m> a group element, <m>g^{\frac{1}{k}}</m> is
    implicitly asking if there is another group element <m>h</m> such
    that <m>h^k = g</m>. (This is almost identical to the logarithm
    problem, but note the difference: in the logarithm, I am asking for
    the unknown exponent, which is an integer; and in the root
    problem, I am asking for the unknown base, this is a group
    element.) Like the logarithm, there is no guarantee that any roots
    exists in general for groups. 
  </p>
  <p>
    All of this discussion of exponents is done with multiplicative
    notations. However, some groups are written with addition. The
    laws still work there, but they look a little different. Iterative
    addition is written <m>ng = g + g + g + \ldots + g</m>.
    I can write the additive versions of exponent laws
    (inlcuding the one that assumes the group is abelian -- additive
    notation is exclusively used for abelian groups, so that
    assumption is safe here.) Again, <m>g,h \in G</m> and <m>m, n \in
    \ZZ</m>.
    <md>
      <mrow>
        (mg) + (ng) = (m+n) g 
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        n(mg) = (nm)g 
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        m(g + h) = mg + mh 
      </mrow>
    </md>
    These are all pretty familiar rules. The first and third look like
    distributive laws. But be careful: these are only rules about
    iterated addition, and the distribution is only for integers. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I'll finish this section by talking, at some length, about
    conjugation. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g, h, k \in G</m> be elements of a group. The
        <term>conjugation</term> of <m>h</m> by <m>g</m> is the
        group element calculated by the expression <m>ghg^{-1}</m>. If
        <m>k = ghg^{-1}</m> for some <m>g</m>, then we say that
        <m>h</m> and <m>k</m> are <term>conjugate</term>. (There is
        some inconsistency in mathematics for the definition of
        conjugacy. Some sources will define the conjugate as
        <m>g^{-1}hg</m>, with the inverse on the left. In these notes,
        I'll be consistent and always keep the inverse on the right.) 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    That conjugacy is only interesting for non-abelian groups. If
    the group is abelian, then <m>ghg^{-1} = gg^{-1}h = h</m>, so
    conjugation doesn't do anything, and only <m>h</m> itself is the only
    group element which is conjugate with <m>h</m>. However, for
    non-abelian groups, conjugacy leads to some pretty interesting
    structural information. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group. Then conjugacy defines a relation on
        the group: two elements are related if they are conjugate. This
        is an equivalence relation.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I need to prove the three properties of an equivalent
        relation. Let me start with reflexive: elements need to be
        conjugate to themselves. Consider this equation.
        <md>
          h = h\Id = h(hh^{-1}) = hhh^{-1}
        </md>
        This equations shows that <m>h</m> is conjugate to itself (via
        conjugation by <m>h</m>), so reflexivity is proved. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Next is symmetry. If <m>h</m> is conjugate to <m>k</m>, I need
        to also prove that <m>k</m> is conjugate to <m>h</m>. The
        assumption here means that there exists <m>g</m> such that
        this equation holds. 
        <md>
          h = gkg^{-1}
        </md>
        Now I'll multiply by <m>g</m> on the right and by
        <m>g^{-1}</m> on the left and simplify. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            h = gkg^{-1}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}hg = g^{-1}gkg^{-1}g 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            g^{-1}hg = \Id k \Id = k
          </mrow>
        </md>
        This shows that <m>k</m> is also conjugate to <m>h</m> (via
        conjugation by <m>g^{-1}</m>), finishing the proof of symmetry. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, I need transitivity. Assume that <m>h</m> is
        conjugate to <m>k</m> and also <m>k</m> is conjugate to
        <m>l</m>. That means there exist two groups elements
        <m>g_1</m> and <m>g_2</m> with these two equations. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            h = g_1kg_1^{-1}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            k = g_2lg_2^{-1}
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Then I replace <m>k</m> in the first equation with its
        expression from the second. 
        <md>
          h = g_1(g_2 l g_2^{-1}) g_1^{-1} = g_1 g_2 l g_2^{-1}
          g_1^{-1} 
        </md>
        Then I use <xref ref="proposition-inverse-product"/>, which I
        can apply to the last two elements of this equation.
        <md>
          h = g_1 g_2 l (g_1g_2)^{-1} 
        </md>
        This prove that <m>h</m> is conjugate to <m>l</m>, via
        conjugation of <m>l</m> by the group element <m>(g_1g_2)</m>. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Since conjugacy is an equivalence relation, I can partition a
        group using this relation. The partitions of a group under
        this relation are called the <term>conjugacy classes</term> of
        the group. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    I'll run through a few examples here to make conjugation
    hopefully a bit clearer. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      In an abelian group, all elements are only conjugate to
      themselves. So the conjugacy classes are all singletons: just a
      set containing a single group element. Again, conjugacy doesn't
      really do anything for an abelian group.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      In any group, <m>\{Id\}</m> is always a conjugacy class. Why is
      this so? Well, assume that some other <m>h</m> is conjugate to
      the identity. That would mean the following equation holds for
      some other group element <m>g</m>.
      <md>
        h = g\Id g^{-1}
      </md>
      But I can simplify this. 
      <md>
        h = g\Id g^{-1} = gg^{-1} = \Id 
      </md>
      This means <m>h</m> is also the identity, so only the identity
      is conjugate to the identity. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example xml:id="example-d4-conjugacy-classes">
    <p>
      The conjugacy classes of <m>D_4</m> are the following sets.
      <md>
        \{\Id\}, \quad 
        \{R_1, R_3\}, \quad 
        \{R_2\}, \quad 
        \{F_1, F_3\}, \quad 
        \{F_2, F_4\}
      </md>
      Feel free to verify this: doing so consists of a lot of
      calculations, either directly or by using the Caley table. For
      example, taking all the conjugates of <m>R_1</m> (that is,
      conjugates by all possible other group elements), you will find
      that you only produce <m>R_1</m> or <m>R_3</m>, and likewise
      for <m>R_3</m>. That justifies the second class, and the rest
      are justified similarly.
    </p>
    <p>
      I can say more about the conjugacy classes of <m>D_4</m>. The
      identity is always its own class, as the previous example
      established in general. For the rotations, the classes
      separate the rotations of order 4 from the single rotation of
      order 2. Then, the classes of the reflections separate the
      reflections that go through two vertices from the reflections
      that go through the middle of two opposite edges. This is
      intersting: the conjugacy classes seem to separate the group
      into sets of transformations which share some properties. This
      doesn't always happen perfectly, but this pattern is,
      thematically, what we hope to see from conjugacy. Conjugacy
      classes, hopefully, should be groups of groups elements with
      similar properties. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This separation into conjugacy classes helps to understand the
      <emph>structure</emph> of <m>D_4</m>. Once we have defined
      groups, pure mathematicians want to understand the structure of
      groups. That's a tricky term: what exactly is a
      <sq>structure</sq>? It's hard to say precisely, since the term
      is used informally. But I can say that the conjugacy classes are
      a structure. If I know the conjugacy classes, I know something
      valuable and interesting about the group: how it breaks down,
      how many classes exist, what common properties are held inside
      each class. This is exactly the kind of thing that we want to
      accomplish in abstract mathematics: buildling analytic tools to
      understand an abstract definition, investigate how it works, and 
      distringuish between different kinds of instances of the
      definition. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    In that spirit, and also to demonstrate some interesting proofs,
    here are some propositions about conjugacy classes. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>g,h \in G</m> be an element of a group. Then the order
        of <m>h</m> is unchanged by conjugation by <m>g</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        There are two cases here: finite and infinite order. I'll
        start with finite order. Let <m>d</m> be the order of
        <m>h</m>: the smallest positive integer such that <m>h^d =
        \Id</m>. Then consider this calculation.
        <md>
          <mrow>
            (ghg^{-1})^d \amp = (ghg^{-1}) (ghg^{-1}) \ldots
            (ghg^{-1}) 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = gh (g^{-1}g) h (g^{-1}g) h \ldots h
            (g^{-1}g) h g^{-1} 
          </mrow>
            \amp = g h \Id h \Id h \ldots h \Id h g^{-1}
          <mrow>
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = gh^d g^{-1}  = gg^{-1} = \Id
          </mrow>
        </md>
        What am I using here? First I am using associativity to change
        the brackets. Then I am cancelling with inverses to make the
        identity. Finally, I am using the fact that <m>h^d = \Id</m>.
        With this calculation, I show that the order of the
        conjugation of <m>h</m> is at most <m>d</m>. But could there
        be a smaller number <m>c</m> such that <m>(ghg^{-1})^c =
        \Id</m>? We'll, let me use <m>c</m> and repeat the first three
        steps of the previous calculation to get this equation.
        <md>
          \Id = (ghg^{-1})^c = g h^c g^{-1} 
        </md>
        Then I can multiply by <m>g</m> on the left and <m>g^{-1}</m>
        on the right. 
        <md>
          g^{-1} \Id  g = g^{-1} g h^c g^{-1} g 
        </md>
        After cancelling, I get this. 
        <md>
          \Id = h^c 
        </md>
        If <m>c \lt d</m>, this contradicts that <m>d</m> is the order
        of <m>h</m>, which cannot be true. (This is, yet again,
        another mini-use of proof by contradiction.) Therefore,
        <m>d</m> is also the smallest positive integer such that
        <m>(ghg^{-1})^d = \Id</m>, so it is the order of the
        conjugation and the proof is completed for the finite case. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Now for the infinite case. <m>h</m> has an infinite order is
        there is no <m>d \in \NN</m> such that <m>h^d = \Id</m>.
        Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exists some
        <m>d</m> such that <m>(ghg^{-1})^d = \Id</m>. I can then
        repeat the previous calculation once more; like the second
        part of the finite case, this would result in <m>h^d =
        \Id</m>. But this contradicts the fact that <m>h</m> has
        infinite order. So, by contradiction, no such <m>d</m> can
        exist and I can conclude that <m>ghg^{-1}</m> also has
        infinite order. 
      </p>
      <p>
        That's both possible cases: finite or infinite order. In both
        cases, the order of the conjugation is the same as the
        original order, so the proof is completed. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Now I want to say something about conjugation in permutation
    groups. 
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-conjugate-cycles">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Any two cycles of the same length in <m>S_n</m> are conjugate
        to each other. More specifically, let <m>(a_1 a_2 \ldots
        a_k)</m> be a cycle and <m>g \in S_n</m>. Then the conjugate of
        the cycle by <m>g</m> is the cycle <m>(g(a_1) g(a_2) \ldots
        g(a_k)</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The <sq>more specifically</sq> is, indeed, the way to to prove
        the general proposition. Consider the conjugation in question.
        <md>
          g (a_1 a_2 \ldots a_k) g^{-1}
        </md>
        I'm going to specifically determing what this product does, as
        a permutation, to each element of <m>\{1, 2, 3, \ldots n\}</m>
        I work in two cases.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, consider any element <m>b \in \{1, 2, 3 \ldots n\}</m>
        such that <m>g^{-1}(b)</m> does not show up in the original cycle.
        For such elements, I trace the action, moving from right to
        left. Doing so, I see that <m>b</m> is sent to
        <m>g^{-1}(b)</m>, is left unchanged by the cycle (since it
        doesn't show up!), and then is sent to <m>g(g^{-1}(b))</m>,
        which is just <m>b</m>. So these elements are unchanged and
        don't need to show up in a cycle description of the
        conjugation. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Next consider any element such that <m>g^{-1}(b) = a_l</m> for
        some index <m>1 \leq l \leq k</m>. I can write such an element
        as <m> b = g(a_l)</m>. I again trace the path of this element.
        <m>b</m> is sent to <m>a_l</m> by <m>g^{-1}</m>. Then it is
        sent to next item in the cycle: <m>a_{l+1}</m>. (This
        <m>a_{l+1}</m> is actually <m>a_1</m> if <m>l = k</m>, when
        the cycle loops around.) Finally, <m>a_{l+1}</m> is  sent to
        <m>g(a_{l+1})</m> by the last part of the product. All in all,
        <m>g(a_l)</m> is sent to <m>g(a_{l+1})</m>, including looping
        around to <m>g(a_1)</m> when <m>l=k</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        So what have I done here? Only the elements <m>g(a_i)</m> show
        up, and they are sent one step down the index. This is exactly
        a new cycle: 
        <md>
          (g(a_1) g(a_2) \ldots g(a_k)) 
        </md>
        I conclude that the conjugate of the cycle is just another
        cycle of length <m>k</m>. I even know how to calculate
        this new cycle, using the argument of this proof. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This argued that the conjugate of a cycle of length <m>k</m>
        is another cycle of length <m>k</m>. But the proposition said
        that <emph>all</emph> cycles of a fixed length are conjugate.
        Why is this stronger statement true? Well, consider the
        argument I just made. I took any permutation <m>g \in S_n</m>
        an got a cycle where the elements in the cycle were acted on
        by the permutation. But <m>S_n</m> is, by definition, the set
        of all permutations! Therefore, in this way, I can produce any
        cycle of length <m>k</m>: there is some permutation of
        <m>\{1,2,3 \ldots n\}</m> that will take the original cycle
        and product any possible new cycle of the same length using
        conjugation.  Therefore, all cycles of the same length are
        conjugate to each other. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    This lets me state a final result about permutations and
    conjugation. I'll call this a theorem; there is no real different
    between a propositions and theorems other than the fact that a
    theorem sound more important. Therefore, we tend to reserve the
    word for results that seem to carry more weight. This seems like a
    big result about permutations, so a theorem it is!
  </p>
  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Two permutations in <m>S_n</m> are conjugate if and only if
        they have the same lengths of cycles in their decomposition
        into disjoint cycles. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I start with a permutation that is a product of disjoint cycles
        <m>c_i</m>. 
        <md>
          \sigma = c_1 c_2 \ldots c_k
        </md>
        Then I apply conjugation by any permutation <m>g</m>.
        <md>
          g \sigma g^{-1} = g c_1 c_2 \ldots c_k g^{-1} 
        </md>
        Then, since <m>g^{-1}g = \Id</m>, I can squeeze that
        expression betweeen any two cycles without changing anything. 
        <md>
          g \sigma g^{-1} = g c_1 g^{-1} g c_2 g^{-1} \ldots g c_k g^{-1} 
        </md>
        Then I can change the grouping by associativity. 
        <md>
          g \sigma g^{-1} = (g c_1 g^{-1}) (g c_2 g^{-1}) \ldots (g
          c_k g^{-1} )
        </md>
        Now the right side is a bunch of conjugations of cycles. Using
        <xref ref="proposition-conjugate-cycles"/>, the result of
        these conjugations is another product of cycles, all of which
        have the same length as the original cycles. Also, I know the
        entires in all the cycles <m>c_i</m> are disjoint. From the
        proof of the prosition, the entries in the new cycles are
        simply given by the permutation <m>g</m> acting on the
        original entries.  The fact that the original entires are
        disjoint means that all the entries were distinct. The fact
        that a bijective map acts on them means that all the entires
        of the new cycles are still distinct: these are still disjoint
        cycles. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This proves one direction of the theorem: if two permutations
        are conjugate, then they have the same lengths of cycles in
        their decomposition into cycles. What about the reverse? Well,
        if two permutations have the same lengths of cycles in their
        decomposition into disjoint cycles, remember that the entires
        in the cycles are just the numbers <m>\{1, 2, \ldots, n\}</m>.
        To change any patterns of the numbers in the cycles into any
        other pattern is just to apply a permutation of the numbers,
        and the calculation above shows that conjugation by the
        permutation has the desired affect on the cycles. Therefore,
        the reverse implication is also proved. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
</section>
